**Definition 2.1 (unchanged original class).** For a valid base
$b=(X,Y,Z)$ put $r=1-Z$ and

$$
\begin{aligned}
&0<Z<1,\qquad 0<X,Y<r,\qquad
J_b=(\max(0,X+Y-r),\min(X,Y)),\\
&p_b(k)=(r-X-Y+k,X-k,Z,Y-k,k),\qquad
\mathbf d=(1,-1,0,-1,1),\\
&I_b(k)=\sum_{i\in\{0,1,3,4\}}p_b(k)_i^{-1},\qquad
w_b(k)_i=\frac{\mathbf d_i}{p_b(k)_i I_b(k)}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{AC.1}
$$

Index $4$ always means original mode $13$. An original control has finite
duration $T\geq0$, continuous real coordinates $k,\chi$ on $[0,T]$,
strict $k(t)\in J_b$, finite variation of $\chi$, and either absolute
continuity of both coordinates or the supplier's finite-piecewise-$C^1$
alternative with continuous derivative rates on each closed piece.
The latter also implies absolute continuity, by finite integration and
telescoping. With a common compatible preparation lift $\theta$, the
actual phase is

$$
\theta_i+H_i(t),\qquad
H_i(t)=\int_0^t w_b(k(s))_i\chi'(s)\,ds,
\qquad
\psi_i(t)=\sqrt{p_b(k(t))_i}e^{-i(\theta_i+H_i(t))}.
\tag{AC.2}
$$

Real lifts and all signed integers in $\chi(T)-\chi(0)=2\pi m$ are
retained. Restricting examples to a double wall or a finite catalogue does
not remove other bases, regularity classes, durations or sectors from the
original task.

